/*! \page developer_backend_id_page Backend Identifiers

\ref developer_backend_page         (Prev/Up) &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_own_page  (Next)    &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

PSCF uses C++ templates to define closely analogous classes that use
different computational backends.

\section developer_backend_id_class_sec Backend Identifier Classes

All PSCF classes that are designed to allow the use of more than one 
computational backend are defined as specializations of class templates 
that accept a special kind of class as a template argument. 
A class that is designed to be used in this role, as a template
argument that is used to identify a specific computational backend, 
is referred to here as a backend identifier class. 

PSCF currently defines two such backend identifier classes, named 
Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT, for which:

  - Class Pscf::CPT identifies a C++ CPU backend

  - Class Pscf::CUT identifies a CUDA GPU backend

Template specializations for which Pscf::CPT is a template argument 
thus use the C++ backend, and are used to construct the pscf_rpc 
program. Template specializations for which Pscf:CUT is a template 
argument instead use a CUDA GPU backend, and are used to construct
the pscf_rpg program.  

A template parameter that is designed to take one of these backend 
identifier classes as its argument is sometimes referred to here as 
a backend identifier parameter.  By convention, the symbol "T" is 
always used to denote a backend identifier parameter in a template
definition.

\section developer_backend_id_rp_sec Template Patterns

The Pscf::Rp and Pscf::Prdc namespaces contain definitions of many class 
templates that take a backend identifier class as a template parameter. 
Most of these class templates also take an int template parameter that 
represents the integer dimension of space, usually denoted by D. For 
such classes, the dimension of space D generally appears as the first 
template parameter, and a backend identifier parameter denoted by T 
generally appears as a second parameter.  

The Pscf::Rp::System class template represents the highest level 
object in a SCFT or PS-SCFT calculation. This template is declared 
as:
\code
namespace Pscf {
   namespace Rp {
      template <int D, class T> class System;
   }
}
\endcode
A valid specialization of this template must take an integer argument 
1, 2, or 3 as the argument of parameter D, and must take one of the two 
possible template identifier classes as the argument of parameter T.  
For example, the class
\code
namespace Pscf {
   Pscf::Rp::System <3, CUT>
}
\endcode
is thus a specialization of the Pscf::Rp::System class template that is 
designed to use a CUDA backend to treat systems with three-dimensional 
(D=3) periodic boundary conditions.

\section developer_backend_id_enum_sec BackendId Enum Class

PSCF also defines an enum class known as Pscf::BackendId for which
different enum values represent different backends.  This enum class is 
defined in the file src/pscf/backend/BackendId.h as follows:
\code
namespace Pscf {

   enum class BackendId {
      Cpp, Cuda
   };

}
\endcode
The enum value BackendId::Cpp represents the C++  CPU backend, while 
BackendId::Cuda represents the Cuda Gpu backend.

The backend identifier classes Pscf::CPT and Pscf::CUT each define a 
const static member variable named "id" that is an instance of BackendId. 
The value of the const member variable CPT::id is BackendId::Cpp, while 
the value of CUT::id is BackendId::Cuda.  

A backend identifier class and a value of the BackendId enum are designed 
to identify a choice of backend in different contexts. A backend 
identifier class is used at compile time, as a label for template 
specializations that use a particular backend.  The value of a variable 
of type BackendId can instead be tested within a function template to 
identify the chosen backend at run time.  In template code that uses a 
template parameter T to represent a backend identifier class, the value 
of T::id can be tested to identify the backend at run time.

<BR>
